  "holdout_metric": null,
  "surprise": "663 at 8 loops (698/653/639) against 675: training each proof only at loop counts >= its discovery depth did not raise the deep computation, it only lowered the shallow one. The mechanism check went as predicted on the shallow side and failed on the deep side. Last-round RL accuracy at 1 loop fell from 500's 0.708/0.691/0.612 to 0.652/0.560/0.585 and the 1-vs-8 gap roughly doubled (0.048 to 0.092 mean, predicted >= 0.10), but accuracy at 8 loops did not rise: 0.733/0.664/0.675 against 500's 0.763/0.734/0.659, and the 4-vs-8 plateau is untouched (0.731 vs 0.733, 0.657 vs 0.664, 0.673 vs 0.675; 500: 0.763 vs 0.763). Eval told the same story: metric minus diag widened from +18 to +33 (28/35/38, predicted >= 35), entirely from the 2-loop diag falling to 630 (670/618/601, 500: 684/667/621), while per-sample dev accuracy at 8 loops was 0.473/0.438/0.420 against 500's 0.481/0.473/0.416. So the one-pass constraint was not what capped depth: freeing the core from reproducing deep-found proofs at 1 loop did not let 8 loops do more than 4, which is the spike's plateau again. Depth-8-only records were 12-17% of RL records in round 1 and 3-5% later; depth-1 records grew from 32-46% to 73-78% as the fine-tuned model re-found proofs shallow, so the curriculum thinned out by itself. Cost of the rule: the weaker shallow model found fewer proofs in the cheap 1-loop calls (seed 0 round 2: 539 targets at 1 loop against 500's 691), cumulative RL solved fell to 1112 (1157/1083/1097) from 1149, and seed 1 lost its 5th round by the 1.15x margin (370 s left, 348 s rounds), which explains most of its -33. Pretraining unchanged (val 0.089/0.085/0.084). Next: the shared core at 4 and 8 loops computes the same thing whatever the fine-tune asks; a depth signal (iteration embedding) or a pretraining loop distribution that rarely demands shallow depth would test whether the core can learn a non-converged iteration at all.",
